<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
  xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">

  <!--
    A library manifest only contributes children of <application>; a bare
    <service> at the top level fails resource linking with
    "unexpected element <service> found in <manifest>".
  -->
  <application>

    <!--
      Only one service receives `com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT`, and Firebase
      documents that the **first declared** wins: `android:priority` is not a contract.
      The merged manifest declared three, ours last:

        expo.modules.notifications.service.ExpoFirebaseMessagingService   priority -1     1st
        com.google.firebase.messaging.FirebaseMessagingService            priority -500   2nd
        expo.modules.veoverlay.VeFirebaseMessagingService                 priority  1     3rd

      Declaring `android:priority="1"` and trusting it left this service permanently
      unused: it was never started, so the offer push was never seen, no launch was
      ever requested, and the only way to reach an offer stayed the notification.
      The competitors are therefore removed outright, which is the remedy Firebase
      documents. Both declare nothing but this one action, so removing them costs
      nothing else.

      Our service extends ExpoFirebaseMessagingService and calls `super`, so
      presentation is untouched — only the class that receives the message changes.
      Notification presentation itself lives in `NotificationsService`, a separate
      receiver that is left alone.
    -->
    <service
      android:name="expo.modules.notifications.service.ExpoFirebaseMessagingService"
      tools:node="remove" />
    <service
      android:name="com.google.firebase.messaging.FirebaseMessagingService"
      tools:node="remove" />

    <!--
      Being the resolved service is what makes the background activity launch
      possible: the decision cannot be taken in JS, which is not consulted when a
      push arrives while the app is in the background.
    -->
    <service
      android:name="expo.modules.veoverlay.VeFirebaseMessagingService"
      android:exported="false">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="com.google.firebase.MESSAGING_EVENT" />
      </intent-filter>
    </service>

  </application>

</manifest>
